      {
        "heading": "Account and Group Lifecycle: Reading a Backdoor in Four Events",
        "content": "Some of the highest-value events in the entire Windows Security log are the ones that track **accounts and group membership** — because creating an account and granting it privilege is exactly how attackers establish durable persistence and escalate. These events are quiet individually (IT does this all day), so the skill is reading them as a *sequence*.\n\nThe events you must recognise, and what each one means:\n\n- **4720 — a user account was created.** A brand-new account appearing, often with an innocuous, service-like name (`svc-backup`, `helpdesk2`).\n- **4728 / 4732 / 4756 — a member was added to a privileged group.** **4732** is a *local* group (e.g. the local Administrators), **4728** a *global* group (e.g. **Domain Admins**), **4756** a *universal* group. Membership in Administrators, Domain Admins, or Enterprise Admins is the keys to the kingdom.\n- **4672 — special privileges assigned at logon.** When the account logs on and is granted admin-equivalent rights, this confirms it now actually wields power.\n- Supporting cast: **4722** (enabled), **4725** (disabled), **4726** (deleted), **4738** (account changed), **4767** (unlocked).\n\nThe classic backdoor is these events **in sequence**: `4720` (create) → `4732`/`4728` (elevate) → `4672` (use), by one actor, minutes apart. No single event is an alert — the **correlation** is the finding, and it is far stronger when it happens outside normal change-control hours or when the actor is not a legitimate IT admin.\n\nTwo habits make these events pay off:\n\n- **Watch the privileged groups specifically.** An addition to `Administrators`, `Domain Admins`, or `Enterprise Admins` deserves attention every single time — these are the memberships that turn a foothold into total control, and legitimate additions to them are rare and change-controlled.\n- **Correlate the actor and the target.** The interesting cases are where the account *doing* the change is not one that should be managing privileged groups, or where the account *being* elevated was created moments earlier. Persistence and privilege escalation are written plainly in this handful of event IDs — you just have to read them as a story, not as isolated lines.",
        "image": {"src":"/lesson-images/wineventlog/account-lifecycle-backdoor.svg","alt":"A diagram showing a backdoor admin account written in four Windows Security events. Event 4720: a user account is created, a new local or domain account with an innocuous name like svc-backup. Event 4732 or 4728: the account is added to a privileged group — 4732 for a local group like Administrators, 4728 for a global group like Domain Admins. Event 4672: special privileges assigned at logon, confirming the new account now has admin-equivalent rights. Read as one story: alone each event is routine IT, but in sequence minutes apart from one actor it is a fresh admin backdoor for persistence and privilege. The diagram stresses that account creation and group changes happen legitimately every day so no single one is an alert; the signal is the correlation of create then elevate then use by the same actor in a tight window, especially outside change-control hours. It lists the high-value account events: 4720 created, 4722 enabled, 4725 disabled, 4726 deleted, 4738 changed, 4728/4732/4756 added to a group, 4672 special privileges — and to watch especially for additions to Administrators, Domain Admins, and Enterprise Admins.","caption":"A backdoor admin reads as four events in sequence — 4720 create → 4728/4732 elevate → 4672 use. No single event alerts; the correlation is the finding.","credit":"Figure authored for this course."}
      },
      {
        "heading": "Centralizing the Logs: WEF, WEC, and Why Local Logs Die With the Host",
        "content": "Everything you have learned about reading Windows events assumes you can actually *get to* them — but by default those events live only on the machine that generated them, in a local log of finite size. That default is a serious operational problem, and understanding why is essential to trusting your own investigations.\n\nThree weaknesses make purely-local logs unreliable for security:\n\n- **They die with the host.** If a machine is wiped, ransomed, or simply reimaged, its local logs are gone — including exactly the evidence you would need to understand what happened to it. An attacker who destroys the box destroys the record.\n- **The attacker can reach them.** A local log sits on a machine the attacker may control; from there they can clear it (Event 1102), shrink it so old entries roll off, or disable auditing — the tampering you met in the log-fundamentals lesson.\n- **They roll over.** Local logs have a maximum size; when full, the oldest events are overwritten. On a busy host, the window you can look back on may be only hours or days — far shorter than typical attacker dwell time.\n\nThe fix is **log centralization**: forwarding events off each host to a central, protected collector as they are generated. In the Windows world the native mechanism is **Windows Event Forwarding (WEF)**, with a **Windows Event Collector (WEC)** server gathering events from many machines; in practice most SOCs forward into a **SIEM** (which is the ingestion pipeline from the SIEM lesson). Either way, the security wins are decisive:\n\n- **Evidence survives the endpoint.** Once an event is forwarded, wiping or ransoming the host no longer destroys the record — the copy is already safe on the collector.\n- **Tampering is defeated.** An attacker clearing the local log is too late; the events already left. And the *act* of clearing (1102) is itself forwarded as evidence.\n- **You can search across the fleet at once.** Centralized logs let you ask \"did this technique appear on *any* host?\" — the cross-environment view that catches lateral movement, instead of logging into machines one at a time.\n\nThe takeaway for the analyst is a trust check you should apply to your own data: **local-only logging is fragile, and its absence during exactly the interesting window may be tampering rather than truth.** When you rely on Windows events for an investigation, know whether they are being forwarded — because centralized, tamper-resistant collection is what turns \"the logs on that box\" into evidence you can actually count on."
      },
      {
        "heading": "The Volume Problem: Filtering Noise and Building a Repeatable Hunt",
        "content": "A single busy Windows server can generate hundreds of thousands of events a day, and a domain generates billions. The challenge with Windows event analysis is rarely that the evidence is missing — it is that the one event you need is buried in an ocean of routine noise. Learning to cut through that volume, rather than scrolling endlessly, is what separates efficient analysts from overwhelmed ones.\n\nThe core discipline is **filter first, read second** — you decide *what* you are looking for and let the tooling discard everything else:\n\n- **Filter by Event ID.** The fastest cut is by the event IDs that matter for your question — pull just the `4625` failures, or the `4728`/`4732` group changes, instead of reading every logon. Knowing which IDs answer which question (the earlier sections) is what makes this precise.\n- **Filter by field with XPath.** The Windows Event Viewer and `wevtutil` support **XPath queries** that filter on the *contents* of an event — a specific account name, a source IP, a target group — not just its ID. That turns \"show me all logons\" into \"show me logons for this account from outside our network,\" which is the difference between noise and a lead.\n- **Scope by time and host.** Bounding a query to the incident window and the hosts in scope collapses the volume dramatically before you ever start reading.\n\nTwo techniques turn filtering into real hunting:\n\n- **Stacking / frequency analysis.** Instead of reading events one by one, *aggregate* them and look at the counts: which processes are rarest across the fleet, which source made the most failed logons, which account touched the most machines. The **outliers** in a frequency count are where threats hide — a technique that appears on one host out of a thousand is exactly what you want to find. This is the analytic backbone of threat hunting applied to event logs.\n- **Save the hunt so it repeats.** A good filter you built once — the XPath for \"new account added to Domain Admins,\" the stack for \"rare parent-child process pairs\" — should be *saved* (as a SIEM search, a scheduled query, or ideally a detection rule) so it runs automatically next time instead of being rebuilt from memory. A hunt that finds something valuable should graduate into a standing detection.\n\nThe mindset to carry away is that **event analysis is a search problem, not a reading problem.** You will never win by scrolling; you win by knowing which events answer your question, filtering hard on their fields, aggregating to surface the outliers, and turning the good filters into detections that watch for you. The volume is the enemy — precise, repeatable filtering is how you beat it."
      }
